cards.question:
DECISION NEEDED: run scripts/reset-test-data.sql on the production Supabase project, or rule that the e2e residue stays.
CONTEXT: CRIT-11 stopped the e2e suite writing into production. It did not remove what was already there. Production opens /inventar on 128 active products with names like TEST-DASH-mt8ztoqf, under a single category called TEST-Categorie, and gate G9 asks Mihai to complete a full cycle on that screen. Removing them is a destructive operation on the project the client is about to accept on, so CLAUDE.md section 8.6 forbids any terminal applying it and the standing P2-07 convention is that test data is marked cancelled, never deleted. This card is the deliberate exception, authored for the owner and never executed by the executor. The statements are quoted in full below so the decision is made on the actual SQL rather than on a description of it.
IDENTIFICATION METHOD: the CRIT-11 marker, products.sku LIKE 'TEST-%', plus categories.name LIKE 'TEST-%'. Not created_by. Two reasons: created_by exists only on inbound_orders and outbound_issues, so it cannot name a single one of the 128 products the client sees, and P2-13 retires the dev accounts while both created_by and changed_by are ON DELETE SET NULL, so that column is erased by a later card. Orders and issues carry no marker of their own (their references are auto-generated INT-YYYY-NNNN and IES-YYYY-NNNN) and are reached through their lines: an order is in scope only when it has at least one line pointing at a TEST- product AND no line pointing at anything else. Mixed orders are left untouched and printed at the end for the owner.
THE FULL STATEMENT SET, VERBATIM, NINE STATEMENTS, in the order the file runs them, inside one transaction, between a pre-check that prints literal counts per table and a post-check that asserts nothing marked survived:

delete from public.status_history
where (entity_type = 'inbound_order'  and entity_id in (select id from rc_reset_inbound_orders))
   or (entity_type = 'outbound_issue' and entity_id in (select id from rc_reset_outbound_issues));
delete from public.batches
where product_id in (select id from rc_reset_products);
delete from public.outbound_lines
where outbound_issue_id in (select id from rc_reset_outbound_issues)
   or product_id in (select id from rc_reset_products);
delete from public.order_lines
where inbound_order_id in (select id from rc_reset_inbound_orders)
   or product_id in (select id from rc_reset_products);
delete from public.outbound_issues
where id in (select id from rc_reset_outbound_issues);
delete from public.inbound_orders
where id in (select id from rc_reset_inbound_orders);
delete from public.reminders
where product_id in (select id from rc_reset_products);
delete from public.products
where id in (select id from rc_reset_products);
delete from public.categories c
where c.name like 'TEST-%'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.products p where p.category_id = c.id);

OPTIONS: (a) Ivan pastes scripts/reset-test-data.sql whole into the Supabase SQL editor, reads the pre-check counts, and commits if they look right or rolls back if they do not; the pre-check runs inside the same transaction as the deletes so the numbers read are the numbers that were true at delete time. (b) Ivan rules that the rows stay and Mihai accepts on a screen full of TEST- products, which costs nothing technically and costs the demo its credibility. (c) A cancelled-not-deleted variant: mark every TEST- product inactive instead of removing it, which honours the standing convention but leaves roughly 300 rows in every count, join and export forever, and does not clear /inventar because the screen already filters on active and still shows them in the totals.
RECOMMENDATION: (a), run once, after P2-12 has landed and immediately before the first real data is entered, in the same sitting as the P2-13 rotation. The residue is not history: it is the output of a suite that was pointed at the wrong project, and there is no real row among the target set to protect. The file is written to be safe when run late, not only when run today, which is why the mixed-order clause exists.
IMPACT IF UNANSWERED: G9 happens on a screen showing 128 TEST- products, and P2-13 stays blocked behind this card, because rotating credentials before deciding what the database should contain gets the order wrong.
